 - **Entering** puts the walker on the standable ground nearest the orbit camera's focus point (rings 0.5 m apart out to 48 m, then each tile's own ground nearest first), facing the way the orbit camera faced and 5° down. The eye is 1.7 m over the feet; the FOV is 60° (vertical) while walking. The near clip stays Bevy's default 0.1 m, which suits eye height. LODs follow the camera distance as always, so close pieces draw at LOD0.
 - **Ground** is the topmost LOD0 surface of the tile's base, centre and lane edge pieces (the surface scatter stands on: meadow cap, road, beach slope, river banks and bed, the bridge deck), sampled in the tile frame through its rotation. Tiles hidden until their pack arrives are not ground; a tile drawn with placeholders stands at y = 0. The eye eases toward 1.7 m over the ground at 12/s, so kerbs and slopes glide.
-- **Collision.** The walker is a 0.3 m circle. Moves are checked in sub-steps of at most 0.1 m and slide along whatever blocks them (each world axis alone). They stop at: a collider (`<piece>_collider` in the kit GLB, hulled to a convex footprint on the ground) of an obstacle piece (`walk::OBSTACLES`: the houses, the windmill, the barn, the bakery, the hay bales, the cow and sheep, the hero oak, spruces, scattered oaks, rocks, fallen logs and the farm's paddock fence), placed with the hero prop's or scatter instance's transform under the tile's rest transform (wildflowers do not block). A collider of disjoint parts is hulled part by part: the paddock fence blocks as six run boxes, so the walker enters the paddock through its 3 m gate and stands inside it; water deeper than 0.6 m on a sea tile (ocean, coast and river mouth; the surface is `water::tiles::WATER_Y`), so the walker wades into the shallows and stops at the shoreline. Rivers and spring streams are fords (owner, 2026-10-20): over water deeper than 0.6 m the feet hold 0.6 m under the surface, so the walker wades down the bank, crosses the channel with its eye 1.1 m above the water and climbs out on the far bank; a cell with no drawn tile (the map edge or an empty slot), with the body never overhanging it; and a step up of more than 0.4 m (a railing).
